Revision history for Tensor-XS

Unreleased

Compare rounded F64 results numerically in decimal-value tests. Perl builds
with wider NVs, including quadmath, expose the F64 storage rounding when
stringifying values. Decimal and rounded fractional expectations now allow
an absolute error below 1e-12 for the small values used by these tests.
Exact integer and binary-fraction checks remain unchanged.

0.02  2026-09-01

The elementwise arithmetic, comparisons, logical operations, reductions,
in-place updates, transposition, and vector/matrix multiplication kernels now
run in XS over the native tensor buffer.  Results are wrapped without first
materializing a Perl data array.  The existing callback-based map and zip APIs
remain available for callers that explicitly supply Perl callbacks.

This is an experimental in-core distribution containing the initial
pure-Perl Tensor, Vector, Matrix, Scalar, and MNIST classes.  The native
implementation and XS migration plan are recorded in XS_DESIGN.md.

The Tensor storage layer now uses a contiguous native XS representation,
including typed integer and floating-point storage, while the public Perl
classes retain their existing interfaces.  The complete nested test suite is
included in the distribution test target and no longer requires
Test::Exception.
